erers people living with Alzheimer's  women so women seem to have be at higher  risk for developing Alzheimer's even if  you account for the fact that they live  longer we're not really sure why that  might be maybe because they are not  succumbing to cardiovascular disease  like men are at an earlier point in  their lives also when there may be some  hormonal influences women also present  with differ  symptoms so it's important to realize  that women make their diagnosis may be  missed  they may be diagnosed as having anxiety  depression and unfortunately even  hysteria menopause symptoms can mimic  the symptoms of Alzheimer's so it's  important have Alzheimer's  genetics people often think that  genetics is the main determinant of  Alzheimer's and that's absolutely not  the case less than 5% of Alzheimer's  cases are from genetics the majority of  Alzheimer's can be prevented by  lifestyle changes and modifications  simple things like diet make sure that  you're eating a diet that's good for  your heart  I made a Turanian diet which is good for  your heart it's also a good anti  Alzheimer's diet make sure that you are  you know your lifestyle that you're  active  I exercise 30 minutes three times a week  you know and you do those things you're  unlikely to have Alzheimer's you can  prevent up to 60% of Alzheimer's cases  yes is there a test because I think  those old risk factors like high blood  pressure diabetes is there a test that  we could take or that we should take I  think a win so I think everybody over  the age of 50 just like we have baseline  colonoscopies baseline mammograms we  should all have a baseline brain  evaluation which includes a map of our  brain strengths and weaknesses so that  down the road if we forget all of us  forget and most forgetting is normal  then we're not going to get worried we  can actually look back and say look it's  a lot the same as it was ten years ago  nothing to worry about but if there's a  problem we can intervene 